Topic: Application Layer — DNS, HTTP/HTTPS & Email Protocols

Today we cover the major Application Layer protocols. These are comparatively easy marks once you understand the purpose and protocol differences.


---

1️⃣ Application Layer

Application Layer is Layer 7 of the OSI model.

It provides network services to applications.

Important protocols:

Application Layer
       │
 ┌─────┼─────────┐
 │     │         │
DNS   HTTP     SMTP
      HTTPS    POP3
               IMAP
      FTP


---

2️⃣ DNS ⭐

DNS = Domain Name System

Its main purpose:

> Convert a domain name into an IP address.



Example:

www.example.com
       ↓
      DNS
       ↓
93.184.216.34

Humans prefer:

google.com

Computers communicate using:

IP address

DNS connects the two.


---

3️⃣ Why DNS?

Without DNS, users would have to remember IP addresses.

Instead of:

142.250.x.x

we use:

google.com


---

4️⃣ DNS Hierarchy

DNS is hierarchical.

Root
                     │
             ┌───────┴───────┐
            .com            .org
             │
          example
             │
          www

Hierarchy:

Root
 ↓
TLD
 ↓
Authoritative domain/server
 ↓
Host


---

5️⃣ Root DNS Servers

At the top is the:

Root

Root servers help direct queries toward the appropriate Top-Level Domain (TLD) servers.

Examples of TLDs:

.com
.org
.net
.in
.edu


---

6️⃣ DNS Query

Suppose you enter:

www.example.com

A simplified resolution process:

Client
  ↓
Local DNS Resolver
  ↓
Root
  ↓
.com TLD
  ↓
Authoritative DNS
  ↓
IP address

Then the IP is returned to the client.


---

7️⃣ DNS Resolver

Your computer typically sends the DNS query to a recursive resolver.

The resolver performs the necessary lookups and returns the answer.

Client
   ↓
Recursive Resolver
   ↓
DNS hierarchy
   ↓
Answer
   ↓
Client


---

8️⃣ DNS Caching ⭐

DNS results are cached.

Why?

To avoid repeating the complete lookup every time.

First request:
Client → DNS hierarchy → IP

Later:
Client → Cache → IP

This reduces:

DNS traffic

Lookup latency



---

9️⃣ TTL in DNS

DNS records have a TTL (Time To Live) value.

It determines how long a resolver may cache the record.

Example:

TTL = 3600 seconds

means the cached record can normally be retained for about:

\[
\boxed{1\ hour}
\]


---

🔥 10️⃣ DNS Record Types

Important records:

A

Maps hostname → IPv4 address.

example.com → 192.0.2.1

AAAA

Maps hostname → IPv6 address.

CNAME

Alias for another hostname.

MX

Specifies mail exchange servers.

NS

Specifies authoritative name servers.

TXT

Stores text information, commonly used for verification and email-related policies.


---

🧠 11️⃣ DNS Record Table

Record	Purpose

A	IPv4 address
AAAA	IPv6 address
CNAME	Alias
MX	Mail server
NS	Name server
TXT	Text/policy information


🔥 A → IPv4

🔥 AAAA → IPv6

🔥 MX → Mail


---

1️⃣2️⃣ DNS Port

DNS commonly uses:

\[
\boxed{53}
\]

DNS queries commonly use:

UDP port 53

TCP port 53 is also used in situations such as zone transfers and responses requiring TCP.

📌 Don't memorize "DNS = only UDP." That's a common trap.


---

1️⃣3️⃣ HTTP

HTTP = HyperText Transfer Protocol

It is used for communication between:

Web Browser ↔ Web Server

Example:

Browser
   ↓ HTTP request
Web Server
   ↓ HTTP response
Browser


---

1️⃣4️⃣ HTTP is Stateless

HTTP is fundamentally:

\[
\boxed{Stateless}
\]

Meaning:

> Each request is treated independently at the protocol level; HTTP itself does not inherently remember previous requests.



Example:

Request 1 → Response
Request 2 → Response
Request 3 → Response

State can be maintained using mechanisms such as:

Cookies

Sessions

Tokens



---

1️⃣5️⃣ HTTP Request

A simplified HTTP request:

GET /index.html HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com

Important components:

Method

URI/path

Version

Headers

Optional body



---

1️⃣6️⃣ HTTP Methods

GET

Retrieve a resource.

POST

Submit data / request creation or processing.

PUT

Create or replace a resource representation.

PATCH

Partially modify a resource.

DELETE

Delete a resource.


---

🔥 1️⃣7️⃣ HTTP Status Codes

Three major categories:

1xx → Informational
2xx → Success
3xx → Redirection
4xx → Client error
5xx → Server error

Important examples:

Code	Meaning

200	OK
201	Created
301	Moved Permanently
302	Found / temporary redirection
304	Not Modified
400	Bad Request
401	Unauthorized
403	Forbidden
404	Not Found
500	Internal Server Error
503	Service Unavailable



---

🧠 1️⃣8️⃣ 401 vs 403

Common interview question.

401

Authentication is required or failed.

Think:

> Who are you?



403

Server understood the request but refuses to authorize it.

Think:

> I know who you are, but you can't access this.




---

1️⃣9️⃣ HTTP vs HTTPS

HTTPS means:

> HTTP over TLS



HTTPS provides:

Encryption

Authentication

Integrity protection


HTTP
 ↓
TLS
 ↓
TCP
 ↓
IP

For HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2, HTTPS commonly uses TCP + TLS.

HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP.


---

🔥 2️⃣0️⃣ HTTP Default Ports

HTTP:

\[
\boxed{80}
\]

HTTPS:

\[
\boxed{443}
\]


---

2️⃣1️⃣ HTTP Persistent Connection

Older HTTP/1.0 commonly opened a separate TCP connection per request unless keep-alive mechanisms were used.

HTTP/1.1 introduced persistent connections as the default behavior.

TCP Connection
 ↓
Request 1 → Response
 ↓
Request 2 → Response
 ↓
Request 3 → Response

This reduces connection-establishment overhead.


---

2️⃣2️⃣ HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2 vs HTTP/3

HTTP/1.1

Persistent connections

Text-based message format

Requests can be pipelined, though browser usage largely moved away from it


HTTP/2

Important features:

Binary framing

Multiplexing

Header compression

Multiple streams over one connection


HTTP/3

Uses:

\[
\boxed{QUIC}
\]

which runs over:

\[
\boxed{UDP}
\]


---

2️⃣3️⃣ FTP

FTP = File Transfer Protocol

Used for:

> File transfer between client and server.



FTP uses separate control and data connections.

Common control connection:

\[
\boxed{TCP\ 21}
\]

Traditional active-mode data connection:

\[
\boxed{TCP\ 20}
\]

📌 Passive FTP uses dynamically negotiated server ports for data.


---

2️⃣4️⃣ Email Architecture

Important protocols:

SMTP
POP3
IMAP

They don't all do the same thing.


---

2️⃣5️⃣ SMTP

SMTP = Simple Mail Transfer Protocol

Used primarily for:

> Sending/relaying email



Sender
  ↓
SMTP
  ↓
Mail Server

Common ports:

TCP 25 → server-to-server SMTP

TCP 587 → message submission

TCP 465 → commonly used for SMTP over implicit TLS


For GATE, remember the basic role:

\[
\boxed{SMTP=Send}
\]


---

2️⃣6️⃣ POP3

POP3 = Post Office Protocol version 3

Used for:

> Retrieving email from a mail server.



Common port:

\[
\boxed{110}
\]

POP3S commonly uses:

\[
\boxed{995}
\]

POP3 traditionally focuses on downloading messages to the client and can be configured to remove them from the server.


---

2️⃣7️⃣ IMAP

IMAP = Internet Message Access Protocol

Used for:

> Managing and accessing email while keeping mail synchronized with the server.



Common port:

\[
\boxed{143}
\]

IMAPS:

\[
\boxed{993}
\]


---

🔥 2️⃣8️⃣ SMTP vs POP3 vs IMAP

Protocol	Main Function

SMTP	Send/relay email
POP3	Retrieve/download email
IMAP	Access/synchronize email on server


Memory:

> SMTP → Send



> POP3 → Pick up



> IMAP → Internet mailbox synchronization




---

2️⃣9️⃣ FTP vs HTTP

FTP	HTTP

File transfer	Web resources
Separate control/data connections	Request/response
TCP	TCP traditionally; HTTP/3 uses QUIC/UDP
Port 21 control	Port 80
Stateful control connection	Stateless application protocol



